1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,791
Sweetheart, in the fall of two thousand

2
00:00:02,791 --> 00:00:05,653
nineteen, my grandson Ben came home from

3
00:00:05,653 --> 00:00:08,087
school, went straight to his room,

4
00:00:08,087 --> 00:00:10,448
and wouldn't come out for supper.

5
00:00:10,628 --> 00:00:11,731
He was ten.

6
00:00:11,911 --> 00:00:14,183
And when I finally got him to

7
00:00:14,183 --> 00:00:15,280
open the door,

8
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:17,473
he was holding his sneakers,

9
00:00:17,473 --> 00:00:19,667
wrapped in silver duct tape.

10
00:00:19,847 --> 00:00:21,873
His teacher had held one up in

11
00:00:21,873 --> 00:00:23,562
front of the whole class.

12
00:00:23,742 --> 00:00:25,048
The next morning,

13
00:00:25,048 --> 00:00:27,122
I put on my church coat and

14
00:00:27,122 --> 00:00:28,351
went to see her.

15
00:00:28,701 --> 00:00:31,150
Tonight I'm going to tell you what

16
00:00:31,150 --> 00:00:32,878
that teacher said to me,

17
00:00:32,878 --> 00:00:34,390
and what I said back.

18
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:37,417
And what I recognized in her face,

19
00:00:37,417 --> 00:00:40,097
sitting across that little desk,

20
00:00:40,097 --> 00:00:42,357
that made both of us cry in

21
00:00:42,357 --> 00:00:44,450
a fourth-grade classroom.

22
00:00:44,630 --> 00:00:45,552
Stay with me,

23
00:00:45,552 --> 00:00:47,820
honey, because it goes back to a

24
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:50,726
church basement in nineteen eighty-three.

25
00:00:51,076 --> 00:00:53,080
That was a hard year for my

26
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:54,640
son Mark, sweetheart.

27
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:57,797
The factory where he'd worked for eighteen

28
00:00:57,797 --> 00:00:59,710
years closed in the spring,

29
00:00:59,710 --> 00:01:02,262
and by summer the savings were gone.

30
00:01:02,442 --> 00:01:04,463
So Mark and his wife and their

31
00:01:04,463 --> 00:01:06,349
two boys moved in with me on

32
00:01:06,349 --> 00:01:08,909
Maple Drive, while he looked for work.

33
00:01:09,089 --> 00:01:11,275
Six of us in that little house

34
00:01:11,275 --> 00:01:11,712
again.

35
00:01:11,892 --> 00:01:14,087
I didn't mind one bit.

36
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:15,834
But Mark minded.

37
00:01:16,014 --> 00:01:17,279
He was ashamed.

38
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:19,846
And Ben, the younger boy,

39
00:01:19,846 --> 00:01:22,773
had grown two sizes since spring.

40
00:01:22,953 --> 00:01:25,965
His sneakers had split along the side,

41
00:01:25,965 --> 00:01:28,026
and the sole was flapping.

42
00:01:28,206 --> 00:01:29,837
He didn't tell anybody,

43
00:01:29,837 --> 00:01:32,601
honey, because he knew money was tight,

44
00:01:32,601 --> 00:01:34,799
and he didn't want his daddy to

45
00:01:34,799 --> 00:01:35,579
feel worse.

46
00:01:35,759 --> 00:01:37,912
So he found Harold's old roll of

47
00:01:37,912 --> 00:01:40,267
duct tape in the garage and wrapped

48
00:01:40,267 --> 00:01:41,343
them up himself.

49
00:01:41,523 --> 00:01:43,311
He was so proud of that.

50
00:01:43,491 --> 00:01:45,042
He said, look,

51
00:01:45,042 --> 00:01:47,369
Grandma, good as new.

52
00:01:47,719 --> 00:01:50,493
His teacher was a woman named Mrs.

53
00:01:50,673 --> 00:01:51,277
Pruitt.

54
00:01:51,457 --> 00:01:53,072
Strict, they said,

55
00:01:53,072 --> 00:01:54,777
but a good teacher.

56
00:01:54,957 --> 00:01:56,421
And that Tuesday,

57
00:01:56,421 --> 00:01:59,176
when the class was lining up for

58
00:01:59,176 --> 00:02:01,587
recess, she saw Ben's shoes.

59
00:02:01,767 --> 00:02:03,174
And she said,

60
00:02:03,174 --> 00:02:04,797
Ben, come here.

61
00:02:04,977 --> 00:02:07,136
And she made him take one off.

62
00:02:07,316 --> 00:02:08,741
And she held it up,

63
00:02:08,741 --> 00:02:11,141
in front of twenty-six children,

64
00:02:11,141 --> 00:02:12,116
and she said,

65
00:02:12,116 --> 00:02:15,041
class, this is what happens when people

66
00:02:15,041 --> 00:02:17,440
don't take care of their things.

67
00:02:17,791 --> 00:02:19,474
The children laughed,

68
00:02:19,474 --> 00:02:20,356
sweetheart.

69
00:02:20,536 --> 00:02:22,057
Of course they did.

70
00:02:22,237 --> 00:02:23,445
They were ten.

71
00:02:23,625 --> 00:02:24,938
And by lunchtime,

72
00:02:24,938 --> 00:02:27,410
they were calling him Duct Tape.

73
00:02:27,590 --> 00:02:29,651
A boy kicked his foot under the

74
00:02:29,651 --> 00:02:31,647
table to see if the tape would

75
00:02:31,647 --> 00:02:32,245
come off.

76
00:02:32,425 --> 00:02:34,762
And Ben sat in the bathroom for

77
00:02:34,762 --> 00:02:36,044
the whole recess,

78
00:02:36,044 --> 00:02:38,230
so nobody could see his feet.

79
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:40,328
He told me all of this in

80
00:02:40,328 --> 00:02:42,628
a whisper, sitting on his bed,

81
00:02:42,628 --> 00:02:44,853
and he made me promise not to

82
00:02:44,853 --> 00:02:46,003
tell his daddy.

83
00:02:46,353 --> 00:02:49,681
If Grandma's story is touching your heart,

84
00:02:49,681 --> 00:02:51,820
tap the like button for me,

85
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:52,692
sweetheart.

86
00:02:52,872 --> 00:02:56,122
It helps more people find these stories.

87
00:02:56,302 --> 00:02:58,231
I kept that promise,

88
00:02:58,231 --> 00:02:58,810
honey.

89
00:02:58,990 --> 00:03:00,801
I didn't tell Mark.

90
00:03:00,981 --> 00:03:03,106
But I didn't sleep either.

91
00:03:03,286 --> 00:03:05,478
I sat at the kitchen table until

92
00:03:05,478 --> 00:03:06,779
two in the morning,

93
00:03:06,779 --> 00:03:08,355
with my tea going cold,

94
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:10,547
deciding what I was going to say

95
00:03:10,547 --> 00:03:11,506
to that woman.

96
00:03:11,686 --> 00:03:13,752
I was seventy-five years old.

97
00:03:13,932 --> 00:03:16,337
And I was angrier than I'd been

98
00:03:16,337 --> 00:03:17,578
in thirty years.

99
00:03:17,928 --> 00:03:20,604
Around midnight, I went out to the

100
00:03:20,604 --> 00:03:23,281
garage and found that roll of duct

101
00:03:23,281 --> 00:03:25,406
tape on Harold's workbench,

102
00:03:25,406 --> 00:03:27,610
right where Ben had left it.

103
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:29,877
And I stood there holding it,

104
00:03:29,877 --> 00:03:31,820
and I thought about Harold,

105
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:34,627
who fixed everything in this house with

106
00:03:34,627 --> 00:03:36,498
that tape for forty years.

107
00:03:36,678 --> 00:03:38,565
The lawnmower handle,

108
00:03:38,565 --> 00:03:41,171
the mailbox, the garden hose.

109
00:03:41,351 --> 00:03:43,754
Nobody ever laughed at Harold.

110
00:03:43,934 --> 00:03:45,594
They called him handy.

111
00:03:45,774 --> 00:03:48,081
I'll tell you why that mattered so

112
00:03:48,081 --> 00:03:49,234
much in a minute.

113
00:03:49,584 --> 00:03:51,585
I went in the next morning at

114
00:03:51,585 --> 00:03:54,483
seven thirty, before the children arrived.

115
00:03:54,663 --> 00:03:56,568
I wore my good navy coat and

116
00:03:56,568 --> 00:03:58,678
Harold's mother's pearl brooch,

117
00:03:58,678 --> 00:04:01,264
because I wanted to look like somebody

118
00:04:01,264 --> 00:04:03,034
who should be listened to.

119
00:04:03,214 --> 00:04:05,859
The principal was a nice young man,

120
00:04:05,859 --> 00:04:07,521
and he looked nervous.

121
00:04:07,701 --> 00:04:09,914
He brought me down the hall to

122
00:04:09,914 --> 00:04:10,209
Mrs.

123
00:04:10,389 --> 00:04:12,852
Pruitt's classroom, and he said,

124
00:04:12,852 --> 00:04:14,545
I'll let you two talk.

125
00:04:14,725 --> 00:04:16,996
In a minute I'll tell you what

126
00:04:16,996 --> 00:04:18,359
I saw on her desk.

127
00:04:18,709 --> 00:04:19,672
Mrs.

128
00:04:19,852 --> 00:04:21,965
Pruitt was about forty-five,

129
00:04:21,965 --> 00:04:24,606
with her hair pulled back tight and

130
00:04:24,606 --> 00:04:26,644
reading glasses on a chain.

131
00:04:26,824 --> 00:04:28,629
She stood up and shook my hand

132
00:04:28,629 --> 00:04:29,773
like a businessman.

133
00:04:29,953 --> 00:04:32,771
And before I could say a word,

134
00:04:32,771 --> 00:04:34,086
she said, Mrs.

135
00:04:34,266 --> 00:04:36,994
Ruth, I know why you're here.

136
00:04:37,174 --> 00:04:40,304
I believe children need to learn responsibility

137
00:04:40,304 --> 00:04:41,702
for their belongings.

138
00:04:41,882 --> 00:04:44,437
If the shoes were in that condition,

139
00:04:44,437 --> 00:04:46,921
someone wasn't taking care of them.

140
00:04:47,101 --> 00:04:48,866
That's all I was teaching.

141
00:04:49,216 --> 00:04:51,650
I sat down in that tiny chair,

142
00:04:51,650 --> 00:04:54,815
sweetheart, the kind made for children,

143
00:04:54,815 --> 00:04:57,087
with my knees up by my chin.

144
00:04:57,267 --> 00:04:58,859
And I said,

145
00:04:58,859 --> 00:04:59,438
Mrs.

146
00:04:59,618 --> 00:05:03,283
Pruitt, my grandson taped those shoes himself,

147
00:05:03,283 --> 00:05:05,832
because his father lost his job,

148
00:05:05,832 --> 00:05:08,143
and he didn't want to ask for

149
00:05:08,143 --> 00:05:08,860
anything.

150
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,292
He wasn't being careless.

151
00:05:11,472 --> 00:05:13,190
He was being brave.

152
00:05:13,370 --> 00:05:15,450
And you held up his bravery for

153
00:05:15,450 --> 00:05:17,596
twenty-six children to laugh at.

154
00:05:17,947 --> 00:05:20,433
She opened her mouth to argue,

155
00:05:20,433 --> 00:05:20,930
honey.

156
00:05:21,110 --> 00:05:23,344
And that's when I really looked at

157
00:05:23,344 --> 00:05:23,606
her.

158
00:05:23,786 --> 00:05:25,749
Something in her face.

159
00:05:25,929 --> 00:05:27,529
The shape of her chin,

160
00:05:27,529 --> 00:05:30,004
the little gap in her front teeth.

161
00:05:30,184 --> 00:05:31,525
And on her desk,

162
00:05:31,525 --> 00:05:32,447
in a frame,

163
00:05:32,447 --> 00:05:35,129
there was an old photograph of a

164
00:05:35,129 --> 00:05:36,302
church I knew.

165
00:05:36,482 --> 00:05:39,327
First Lutheran, on Third Street.

166
00:05:39,507 --> 00:05:40,563
My church.

167
00:05:40,743 --> 00:05:43,327
Harold and I went there for fifty

168
00:05:43,327 --> 00:05:43,796
years.

169
00:05:43,976 --> 00:05:46,098
I said, did you grow up in

170
00:05:46,098 --> 00:05:46,914
Mansfield?

171
00:05:47,094 --> 00:05:48,777
She said, yes.

172
00:05:48,957 --> 00:05:49,747
Why?

173
00:05:50,097 --> 00:05:51,800
So far, the duct tape,

174
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:53,968
the classroom, the laughing,

175
00:05:53,968 --> 00:05:56,833
and a stern teacher with a photograph

176
00:05:56,833 --> 00:05:58,769
of my church on her desk.

177
00:05:58,949 --> 00:06:01,282
I said, what was your name before

178
00:06:01,282 --> 00:06:02,130
you married?

179
00:06:02,310 --> 00:06:03,439
And she said,

180
00:06:03,439 --> 00:06:04,133
Kessler.

181
00:06:04,313 --> 00:06:05,799
Linda Kessler.

182
00:06:05,979 --> 00:06:07,777
And my heart stopped,

183
00:06:07,777 --> 00:06:08,719
sweetheart.

184
00:06:08,899 --> 00:06:11,697
Because I remembered a Linda Kessler.

185
00:06:11,877 --> 00:06:14,117
A skinny little girl with a gap

186
00:06:14,117 --> 00:06:15,056
in her teeth,

187
00:06:15,056 --> 00:06:17,512
in the basement of First Lutheran,

188
00:06:17,512 --> 00:06:20,329
at Christmas, in nineteen eighty-three.

189
00:06:20,509 --> 00:06:21,589
Stay with me.

190
00:06:21,939 --> 00:06:24,074
Every December back then,

191
00:06:24,074 --> 00:06:26,466
our church ran a shoe drive.

192
00:06:26,646 --> 00:06:29,339
The ladies collected money all year,

193
00:06:29,339 --> 00:06:31,658
and we bought new shoes for any

194
00:06:31,658 --> 00:06:33,903
child in town who needed them.

195
00:06:34,083 --> 00:06:35,901
I was in charge of it for

196
00:06:35,901 --> 00:06:36,846
twelve years.

197
00:06:37,026 --> 00:06:39,083
We set up in the church basement

198
00:06:39,083 --> 00:06:41,462
with a foot measuring stick and boxes

199
00:06:41,462 --> 00:06:42,940
stacked to the ceiling,

200
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:45,191
and the children came in with their

201
00:06:45,191 --> 00:06:46,476
mothers, one by one.

202
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:48,759
And in nineteen eighty-three,

203
00:06:48,759 --> 00:06:50,790
a little girl came in alone.

204
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:52,602
She was about nine.

205
00:06:52,782 --> 00:06:54,490
Her mother hadn't come,

206
00:06:54,490 --> 00:06:57,310
she said, because her mother was sick.

207
00:06:57,490 --> 00:06:59,766
And when she sat down and I

208
00:06:59,766 --> 00:07:01,283
unlaced her shoes,

209
00:07:01,283 --> 00:07:04,148
they were wrapped in masking tape,

210
00:07:04,148 --> 00:07:04,654
honey.

211
00:07:04,834 --> 00:07:08,131
Masking tape, soaked through from the snow.

212
00:07:08,311 --> 00:07:09,739
Her socks were wet.

213
00:07:09,919 --> 00:07:11,777
Her little toes were red.

214
00:07:11,957 --> 00:07:13,030
And she said,

215
00:07:13,030 --> 00:07:16,333
very quietly, please don't tell anybody.

216
00:07:16,513 --> 00:07:18,359
I don't want them to know.

217
00:07:18,710 --> 00:07:20,660
I didn't tell anybody.

218
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:22,971
I took her into the back room,

219
00:07:22,971 --> 00:07:25,031
away from the other children.

220
00:07:25,211 --> 00:07:27,409
I dried her feet with a dish

221
00:07:27,409 --> 00:07:29,684
towel and put two pairs of my

222
00:07:29,684 --> 00:07:31,411
own wool socks on her.

223
00:07:31,591 --> 00:07:33,744
And I gave her the best pair

224
00:07:33,744 --> 00:07:34,974
of boots we had,

225
00:07:34,974 --> 00:07:35,974
the red ones,

226
00:07:35,974 --> 00:07:37,511
with the fur lining,

227
00:07:37,511 --> 00:07:39,126
that I'd been saving.

228
00:07:39,306 --> 00:07:40,238
And I said,

229
00:07:40,238 --> 00:07:42,951
honey, there's no shame in tape.

230
00:07:43,131 --> 00:07:45,070
Tape means you're making do.

231
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:48,176
Making do is the bravest thing there

232
00:07:48,176 --> 00:07:48,420
is.

233
00:07:48,770 --> 00:07:51,606
I remembered her because she didn't smile

234
00:07:51,606 --> 00:07:52,989
once the whole time,

235
00:07:52,989 --> 00:07:53,750
sweetheart.

236
00:07:53,930 --> 00:07:56,636
Most children were so excited about new

237
00:07:56,636 --> 00:07:59,619
shoes that they danced around the basement.

238
00:07:59,799 --> 00:08:02,580
Linda just sat very still and looked

239
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:03,584
at the floor.

240
00:08:03,764 --> 00:08:05,957
When I laced up the red boots,

241
00:08:05,957 --> 00:08:08,516
she touched the fur inside with one

242
00:08:08,516 --> 00:08:11,148
finger, like she was afraid it would

243
00:08:11,148 --> 00:08:11,879
disappear.

244
00:08:12,059 --> 00:08:13,408
And when she left,

245
00:08:13,408 --> 00:08:15,955
she walked out the door backwards,

246
00:08:15,955 --> 00:08:18,352
so she could keep looking at me.

247
00:08:18,532 --> 00:08:20,657
I thought about her for years.

248
00:08:21,007 --> 00:08:24,017
I looked at that teacher across the

249
00:08:24,017 --> 00:08:25,479
desk, and I said,

250
00:08:25,479 --> 00:08:28,059
Linda, did you have red boots,

251
00:08:28,059 --> 00:08:29,435
with fur inside?

252
00:08:29,615 --> 00:08:31,452
And her face changed,

253
00:08:31,452 --> 00:08:32,413
sweetheart.

254
00:08:32,593 --> 00:08:35,159
All that hardness just fell away.

255
00:08:35,339 --> 00:08:37,243
She took off her glasses.

256
00:08:37,423 --> 00:08:39,535
She said, how do you know about

257
00:08:39,535 --> 00:08:40,488
the red boots?

258
00:08:40,668 --> 00:08:41,628
And I said,

259
00:08:41,628 --> 00:08:44,418
because I put them on your feet.

260
00:08:44,598 --> 00:08:47,129
In the back room of First Lutheran.

261
00:08:47,309 --> 00:08:50,131
You had masking tape on your shoes.

262
00:08:50,481 --> 00:08:53,143
She put both hands over her face,

263
00:08:53,143 --> 00:08:53,627
honey.

264
00:08:53,807 --> 00:08:57,023
And she cried like that nine-year-old girl.

265
00:08:57,203 --> 00:08:59,763
She said, I wore those boots for

266
00:08:59,763 --> 00:09:00,883
three winters.

267
00:09:01,063 --> 00:09:03,176
Until my toes curled.

268
00:09:03,356 --> 00:09:06,177
I never told anybody about the tape.

269
00:09:06,357 --> 00:09:07,809
Not my husband.

270
00:09:07,989 --> 00:09:09,486
Not anybody.

271
00:09:09,666 --> 00:09:12,616
I promised myself nobody would ever look

272
00:09:12,616 --> 00:09:14,460
at me and see a poor girl

273
00:09:14,460 --> 00:09:14,902
again.

274
00:09:15,082 --> 00:09:17,028
And then she said,

275
00:09:17,028 --> 00:09:17,892
oh, God.

276
00:09:18,072 --> 00:09:20,046
I did that to your grandson.

277
00:09:20,226 --> 00:09:22,395
I did the exact thing I was

278
00:09:22,395 --> 00:09:23,198
afraid of.

279
00:09:23,548 --> 00:09:26,102
I'll tell you something I've learned in

280
00:09:26,102 --> 00:09:27,739
eighty years, sweetheart.

281
00:09:27,919 --> 00:09:30,700
People who were ashamed as children are

282
00:09:30,700 --> 00:09:33,624
sometimes the hardest on the children who

283
00:09:33,624 --> 00:09:34,908
remind them of it.

284
00:09:35,088 --> 00:09:36,935
It isn't cruelty,

285
00:09:36,935 --> 00:09:37,805
usually.

286
00:09:37,985 --> 00:09:39,099
It's fear.

287
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:42,017
When Linda saw that tape on Ben's

288
00:09:42,017 --> 00:09:44,422
shoes, she wasn't seeing Ben.

289
00:09:44,602 --> 00:09:46,541
She was seeing herself.

290
00:09:46,721 --> 00:09:48,567
And she wanted it gone.

291
00:09:48,747 --> 00:09:51,448
I took her hand across that little

292
00:09:51,448 --> 00:09:53,990
desk, and we both just sat there

293
00:09:53,990 --> 00:09:54,785
and cried.

294
00:09:55,135 --> 00:09:57,640
That afternoon, honey,

295
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:58,095
Mrs.

296
00:09:58,275 --> 00:10:00,678
Pruitt did something I'll never forget.

297
00:10:00,858 --> 00:10:03,209
She asked Ben to stay in at

298
00:10:03,209 --> 00:10:03,819
recess.

299
00:10:03,999 --> 00:10:06,274
And she told him she was sorry.

300
00:10:06,454 --> 00:10:08,442
And then, the next morning,

301
00:10:08,442 --> 00:10:10,504
in front of the whole class,

302
00:10:10,504 --> 00:10:12,638
she told them a story about a

303
00:10:12,638 --> 00:10:15,289
little girl with masking tape on her

304
00:10:15,289 --> 00:10:17,424
shoes, and a lady in a church

305
00:10:17,424 --> 00:10:19,191
basement with red boots.

306
00:10:19,371 --> 00:10:20,615
And at the end,

307
00:10:20,615 --> 00:10:23,434
she said, that little girl was me.

308
00:10:23,784 --> 00:10:26,326
She said, Ben is the bravest boy

309
00:10:26,326 --> 00:10:27,359
in this room.

310
00:10:27,539 --> 00:10:30,906
He fixed something himself instead of complaining.

311
00:10:31,086 --> 00:10:33,428
And anybody who calls him Duct Tape

312
00:10:33,428 --> 00:10:35,301
again will be talking to me.

313
00:10:35,481 --> 00:10:37,486
Ben told me the room was so

314
00:10:37,486 --> 00:10:39,788
quiet you could hear the clock.

315
00:10:39,968 --> 00:10:40,910
And at lunch,

316
00:10:40,910 --> 00:10:43,300
the boy who'd kicked his foot sat

317
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:45,472
down next to him and asked him

318
00:10:45,472 --> 00:10:47,573
how he wrapped them so tight.

319
00:10:47,753 --> 00:10:49,622
They're still friends today.

320
00:10:49,972 --> 00:10:51,376
And that Christmas,

321
00:10:51,376 --> 00:10:54,703
sweetheart, Linda Pruitt opened a shoe closet

322
00:10:54,703 --> 00:10:55,811
at that school.

323
00:10:55,991 --> 00:10:59,025
A little room by the nurse's office,

324
00:10:59,025 --> 00:11:01,806
full of new sneakers and boots in

325
00:11:01,806 --> 00:11:04,755
every size, where any child can go,

326
00:11:04,755 --> 00:11:07,957
quietly, with no questions and no list

327
00:11:07,957 --> 00:11:08,716
of names.

328
00:11:08,896 --> 00:11:10,941
She asked if she could name it

329
00:11:10,941 --> 00:11:11,555
after me.

330
00:11:11,735 --> 00:11:12,885
I said, honey,

331
00:11:12,885 --> 00:11:15,020
name it after your mother.

332
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:16,895
She said, no.

333
00:11:17,075 --> 00:11:18,945
I want the children to know a

334
00:11:18,945 --> 00:11:20,105
stranger did this.

335
00:11:20,456 --> 00:11:22,556
It's called Ruth's Closet,

336
00:11:22,556 --> 00:11:24,252
and it's still there.

337
00:11:24,432 --> 00:11:26,443
Mark found work that winter,

338
00:11:26,443 --> 00:11:27,520
at a warehouse,

339
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,746
and the family moved out in the

340
00:11:29,746 --> 00:11:30,249
spring.

341
00:11:30,429 --> 00:11:31,761
Ben's sixteen now,

342
00:11:31,761 --> 00:11:33,761
and taller than his father.

343
00:11:33,941 --> 00:11:35,977
And every December,

344
00:11:35,977 --> 00:11:37,157
he and Mrs.

345
00:11:37,337 --> 00:11:39,775
Pruitt go to the store together and

346
00:11:39,775 --> 00:11:41,586
fill that closet up again.

347
00:11:41,766 --> 00:11:43,958
He uses his own money from his

348
00:11:43,958 --> 00:11:44,761
summer job.

349
00:11:44,941 --> 00:11:45,958
He told me,

350
00:11:45,958 --> 00:11:49,195
Grandma, I want somebody to get the

351
00:11:49,195 --> 00:11:50,119
red boots.

352
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:53,324
Here's what I want you to remember,

353
00:11:53,324 --> 00:11:53,813
honey.

354
00:11:53,993 --> 00:11:56,773
Before you laugh at somebody's shoes,

355
00:11:56,773 --> 00:11:58,802
think about who taped them,

356
00:11:58,802 --> 00:11:59,403
and why.

357
00:11:59,583 --> 00:12:02,413
Making do is the bravest thing there

358
00:12:02,413 --> 00:12:02,648
is.

359
00:12:02,828 --> 00:12:04,454
Tell me in the comments,

360
00:12:04,454 --> 00:12:06,960
sweetheart, did you ever have to make

361
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:08,924
do with something as a child?

362
00:12:09,104 --> 00:12:11,447
There are more of my family's stories

363
00:12:11,447 --> 00:12:13,664
in the playlist right there on your

364
00:12:13,664 --> 00:12:14,108
screen.

365
00:12:14,288 --> 00:12:15,158
Good night.

366
00:12:15,338 --> 00:12:16,813
Merry Christmas.
